#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# parabolic_cylinder_d

#% text
# `parabolic_cylinder_d(nu, z)` gives the parabolic cylinder function `D_nu(z)`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `D_nu(z)` is the solution of Weber's equation `w'' + (nu + 1/2 - z^2/4) w = 0` that decays like `z^nu*exp(-z^2/4)` as `z` tends to `inf` (DLMF 12.2 with `U(a, z) = D_(-a - 1/2)(z)`).

#% item
# For a non-negative integer `nu` it is `exp(-z^2/4)` times a Hermite polynomial, `2^(-nu/2)*hermite_h(nu, z/sqrt(2))`.

#% item
# `D_0(z)` is `exp(-z^2/4)`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
parabolic_cylinder_d(0, z)
#% output
#= exp((-1/4)*z^2)

#% cell
parabolic_cylinder_d(0, 1.5)
#% output
#= 0.569782824730923

#% cell
parabolic_cylinder_d(0, [0.0, 1.0, 2.0])
#% output
#= [1.0, 0.7788007830714049, 0.36787944117144233]

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# Numerical values are not computed for most orders:

#% cell
parabolic_cylinder_d(0.5, 1.5)
#% output
#= 0.7278946486531516

#% cell
parabolic_cylinder_d(3, 1.2)
#% output
#= -1.3060500824049701


#% text
# Integer orders in closed form:

#% cell
parabolic_cylinder_d(2, x)
#% output
#= -exp((-1/4)*x^2) + exp((-1/4)*x^2)*x^2

#% text
# The value at 0 for any order:

#% cell
parabolic_cylinder_d(nu, 0)
#% output
#= 2^((1/2)*nu)*pi^(1/2)/gamma((1/2)*(1 - nu))

#% text
# Machine values:

#% cell
parabolic_cylinder_d(0.51, 0.87)
#% output
#= 0.8481137305149468

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `hermite_h`, `hypergeometric_u`, `whittaker_w`
